For commands, prebuilt C commands are located in cmd, otherwise you can create commands yourself and insert them into bin.
The Makefile will automatically detect your commands so you don't have to edit anything. Without commands, this will result in a kernel panic.
For building, use 'make' to compile. Once compiling is finished, a bootable kernel image 'tanja-base' will be located at arch/x86/boot.
 ___________________
[ REQUIRED PACKAGES ]
|-------------------|
| 1) make           |
| 2) nasm           |
| 3) gcc            |
|___________________|

For compiling on BSD systems, you should use gmake instead of make,
this is because BSD's make can't use the traditional linker.ld for TanjaOS.
You will see a file called "Makefile.bsd", this is the Makefile for BSD systems.
To use Makefile.bsd, use 'gmake <option> -f Makefile.bsd'. 
 _________________________
[ REQUIRED PACKAGES (BSD) ]
|-------------------------|
| 1) gmake                |
| 2) nasm                 |
| 3) gcc                  |
|_________________________|


TanjaOS C notes
---------------
The compiler exposes standard C library names for the freestanding TanjaOS
environment: putchar(), getchar(), puts(), printf(), scanf(), strlen(),
strcmp(), strncmp(), strcpy(), strncpy(), strcat(), strchr(), strrchr(),
strstr(), memset(), memcpy(), memmove(), memcmp(), atoi(), abs(), rand(),
srand(), and ctype functions such as isdigit(), isalpha(), isalnum(),
isspace(), tolower(), and toupper().

The only TanjaOS-specific C APIs intentionally exposed are clear_screen()
and the cmd_*() shell-command functions. Legacy non-standard helpers such as
print(), print_int(), print_hex(), read_int(), read_line(), min(), max(), and
sleep_ms() are no longer C built-ins.

Every shell command is also callable from C using its cmd_* function:
cmd_mkdir("games"), cmd_ls(""), cmd_echo("hello"), cmd_cp("a b"), etc.

Interactive shell C-style calls are supported too, for example:
cmd_mkdir("games");
cmd_ls("");
